Why not using marked.js or equivalent, plugging it with a Fetch API instead?<p>example:<p><pre><code>  &lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;hello&lt;&#x2F;title&gt;&lt;&#x2F;head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
  &lt;main id=root &gt;&lt;&#x2F;main&gt;
  &lt;script src=.&#x2F;marked.min.js async defer &gt;&lt;&#x2F;script&gt;
  &lt;script&gt;
  async function load() {
    let element = document.querySelector(&quot;main#root&quot;);
    await fetch(&quot;.&#x2F;index.md&quot;) 
      .then(resp =&gt; resp.text())
      .then(text =&gt; marked(text, { ... }))
      .then(html =&gt; {
        element.innerHTML = html;
      });
  }
  window.onload = load;
  &lt;&#x2F;script&gt;
  &lt;&#x2F;body&gt;
  &lt;&#x2F;html&gt;
</code></pre>
since it directly injects the resulting html, you may set &quot;unsafe HTML&quot; option in marked (or markdown.js) and include &lt;title&gt; tags within your markdown document. this will _also_ rewrite the browser title(s)<p>&gt; edit: formatting
